## P1 — matched trigger silently omits configured mandatory follow-up

Location: services/foundation/live_hold.py lines 185–187 (and selected-only iteration immediately after); accepted supplement docs/implementation/g1-live-hold-trigger-binding-supplement-r1-20261021.md line 25.

The accepted contract says: “Each matched binding's follow-up ref resolves in that exact registered package and appears in its trigger's and bundle's follow_up_refs.” The implementation filters binding.follow_ups to those named by trigger.follow_up_refs, then compares only that filtered subset with trigger.follow_up_refs. This verifies absence of extras but not absence of omissions. A matched binding containing F1 and F2 therefore accepts trigger and bundle containing only F1, commits the invalidation and creates only one task. F2's owner/deadline/closure are not enforced for that operation.

Concrete runtime reproduction used the candidate's valid archive/activation/login helpers and an independently written expected-refusal assertion. install_extra_transition_fixture configures one matched rule binding containing exact hold-task-1 (payload SHA256 558205e7652d4a3d91f932d02f736efbdaa54449e2f75633dad862d944f78080) and hold-task-2 (a3678929b0fedb664fa0b0c286d4566917de636eaa2f0f968446751cf6740d80). Its initial enter bundle names only the first. A real authenticated TestClient POST returned HTTP 200 and status committed, one task ID, and database binding count 1. The independent assertion expected HTTP 409 refusal and failed. Exact command, full response, stdout and traceback are preserved in subset-raw.json. This is not an inference from a mocked prepare() function. Candidate fixture assembly is reused; the expected result is derived independently from the accepted clause.

Minimal resolution: compare the complete configured binding follow-up ref set with the matched trigger's ref set before selecting/creating tasks; retain exact bundle-union equality and full closure checks. Refactor the two-transition fixture so genuinely different required follow-up sets have distinct exact trigger-rule bindings, or obtain a separately reviewed explicit contract amendment if the intended semantics are an optional pool. Do not silently reinterpret the accepted mandatory list as a pool. Add both omission-negative and complete-two-follow-up-positive independent cases. Preserve single-increment and retry/history semantics. No repair was made here.

The existing strengthening fixture currently teaches the opposite behavior: tests/integration/replay/live_helpers.py adds change_reason task refs into the initial rule binding, and test_live_hold.py:439–451 installs it. Its subsequent two-transition passing test is therefore useful mechanics evidence but cannot certify this admission law.

